==English==

===Etymology===
Origin uncertain; compare (probably from<ref>[http://www.etymonline.com/index.php?term=simper simper] in [[:w:Online Etymology dictionary|Online Etymology dictionary]]</ref>) {{etyl|da|en}} {{term|simper|lang=da}} / {{term|semper||coy|lang=da}}, German {{term|zimper||elegant, dainty|lang=de}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˈsɪmpə/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˈsɪmpɚ/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-simper.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
*: {{rhymes|ɪmpə(r)|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{context|intransitive|lang=en}} To [[smile]] in a [[foolish]], [[frivolous]], [[self-conscious]], [[coy]], or [[smug]] manner.
#* '''1892''', [[w:Mark Twain|Mark Twain]], ''The American Claimant'', ch. 21:
#*: Why, look at him—look at this '''simpering''' self-righteous mug!
#* '''1915''', [[w:Harold MacGrath|Harold MacGrath]], ''The Voice In The Fog'', ch. 24:
#*: How the fools kotowed and '''simpered''' while I looked over their jewels and speculated upon how much I could get for them!
# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}} To glimmer; to twinkle.
#* Herbert
#*: Yet can I mark how stars above / '''Simper''' and shine.

